  "full_response": "Designing RESTful APIs is a critical part of building scalable and maintainable web services. Here are some of the best practices:\n\n1. **Use nouns to define resources**: In a RESTful API, resources should be clear, unambiguous and resource-agnostic. Sub-resources can be sliced from these root resources using URIs or path parameters.\n\n2. **Predictable URLs structure**: The URL should follow a standard pattern to allow clients to predict how to access the various resources.\n\n3. **Use HTTP methods explicitly**: Each operation on a resource should map to the correct HTTP method (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE) to clearly define the action you are trying to achieve.\n\n4. **empotent methods**: GET requests should not modify the state of the resource. POST creates new resources, PUT/replaces an existing resource without creating a duplicate, and DELETE removes resources. PATCH/partial updates should not create new resources either.\n\n5. **Content negotiation**: Use HTTP headers to let the client inform the server about what response format it prefers.\n\n6. **Versioning**: Make sure you version your API, so that changes can be made without breaking client integrations.\n\n7. **Statelessness**: Every request from any client to any server should contain all what information is necessary, eliminating the need the server to store any session information about the client.\n\n8. **Use status codes explicitly**: HTTP status codes should be correctly to inform the client about the status of the request. For example:\n   - 200 OK for successful GET, PUT or DELETE\n   -201 Created for successful POST\n   - 400 Bad Request if the input doesn’t meet the expectations\n   - 401 Unauthorized for access-related errors\n   - 404 Not Found for requested resources not found, etc.\n\n9. **HATEOAS (Hypermedia as the Engine of Application State)**: Include hypermedia links in your responses to guide the client on what actions are available.\n\n10. **Properly formatted and documented**: Define and document API endpoints with consistent, easily readable formats for endpoints, request/responses bodies, and possible status codes.\n\n11. **Use HTTPS**: Secure your API by using HTTPS to encrypt the data in and protect against interception and man-in-the-middle attacks.\n\n12. **Graceful failure handling**: When an API fails, provide clear, actionable error messages Allow retryable error responses (e.g., “Too many requests”).\n\n13",
